# Radio calibration and setup audit

Source: `openrc-audit` at `f41d0fd2eb5483c609bcd858bcf22776eb311e14`, clean when inspected. Read-only audit; no repository files changed. Severity uses P1 = major safety/correctness exposure, P2 = substantial user-facing defect, P3 = lower-impact contract or usability gap. The observed results below are synthetic, not hardware qualification.

## P2 — recalibration drops AUX controls without a complete warning (high confidence)

- `app/input/rc_calibration.gd:337-353` removes each existing AUX axis that becomes a flight axis, but stores every warning in a single string, overwriting earlier warnings. `app/input/controller_profile.gd:207-225` removes the conflicting bindings when the measured mapping is merged. Intentional removal is safe from axis conflicts, but the user must know every removed control.
- In the Home draft, `app/ui/radio_calibration_setup.gd:165-166` emits only that last string; `app/ui/controller_setup.gd:518-539` shows only the emitted warning. In the managed in-flight fixed-wing and helicopter flows, `app/sim/flight_session.gd:1866-1871` and `app/sim/helicopter_flight_session.gd:984-989` pass only the profile to persistence and report success, never conveying `calibration.aux_warning`. The warnings are pushed to the engine log, but not reported in the Pause result.
- Reproduce: begin with a valid radio profile whose gear uses axis 5 and smoke uses axis 6, while flight uses 0/1/2/3; recalibrate so roll is 5 and pitch is 6. Both controls are absent after merging, while the only returned warning says smoke was disabled. In-flight, even that warning is omitted from the success message. This can leave a previously configured flight-control-related AUX (including gear, arm, or flight mode, depending on activity and binding) unavailable; no claim is made that a specific aircraft or actual transmitter has been tested.
- Evidence: `project/audit_probe.gd`, `runs/calibration-pure-audit-aux-hlu2drsp/{report.json,engine.log}`: 13 checks, 0 failed; two expected warning diagnostics, checked runner `ok=true`. Existing `app/tests/test_controller_aux_runtime.gd:153-167` checks one overlapping AUX is removed but does not check multiple removals or the final warning. Existing persistence checks do not assert the user-visible flight calibration result (`app/tests/test_controller_persistence.gd:115-126`).
- Remediation: return structured IDs for all removed bindings, propagate through both in-flight result paths and Home draft, and prominently require reassignment before using affected controls. Preserve the safe conflict exclusion. Regression: exercise two overlaps, smoke plus a different AUX, on Home and in-flight fixed-wing/helicopter, verify each named and surviving channels preserved.

## P2 — setup's Mode 1 choice is not passed to the calibration view (high confidence)

- `app/ui/controls/setup_assistant.gd:372-395,784-795` allows choosing Mode 1. `app/ui/controls/setup_assistant_bridge.gd:74-79` accepts a radio Mode 1/2 choice but changes no draft, shared setting, or wizard argument. `app/ui/radio_calibration_setup.gd:91-92` chooses the unrelated HUD stick-mode preference; `app/ui/controls/calibration_view.gd:217-236` defaults to Mode 2 when it cannot find a preference. `calibration_view.gd:294-369` uses that value for the targets and labels. The assistant's Test and Summary instead use the local selected mode (`setup_assistant.gd:646-657,958-971,1235-1245`).
- Reproduce: with HUD preference absent/default Mode 2, choose a radio and Mode 1 in the assistant and start calibration. The assistant selection is Mode 1, but the wizard draws the throttle on the left and pitch on the right (Mode 2). The text still says function names, and the radio's channel is learned from movement; the wrong picture does not inevitably corrupt the mapping. A person following the visual positions can, however, be guided toward the wrong controls. Changing the selection also does not persist it as a radio presentation preference for reopening the assistant.
- Evidence: the isolated test uses the actual copied assistant, bridge, wizard, and view with a stub connection. `project/ui_probe.gd`; `runs/calibration-ui-audit-s8fv9m38/{report.json,engine.log}`: `assistant=mode1 wizard_mode=2 left=[yaw,throttle] right=[roll,pitch]`; 5 checks, 0 failed. Existing `app/tests/test_calibration_view.gd:83-99` verifies each layout and HUD lookup separately; `app/tests/test_setup_assistant_wiring.gd:122` exercises a gamepad preset, not the radio assistant-to-wizard handoff.
- Remediation: pass the selected radio mode explicitly to this calibration invocation; keep HUD preference independent unless the user is explicitly choosing to change it. Regression: cover Mode 1 and 2 against opposite saved HUD settings, wizard rest/throttle/pitch targets, and reopening behavior.

## P2 — a single rest reading and a single endpoint outlier can pass as calibrated idle/center (high confidence in software result; physical frequency unknown)

- `app/input/rc_calibration.gd:145-154,167-169` makes one instantaneous rest sample the stored center and tracks absolute minima/maxima across the sweep, without a stability window or outlier rejection. For throttle, lines 195-202 accept a rest-to-extreme difference of 5% of the measured span; centered axes at lines 203-210 allow the returned sample to differ from captured center by up to 10% of the shorter side. The in-range values pass `_valid_axis_sample` at 262-268. `app/ui/radio_calibration_setup.gd:135-143,155-166` considers <=5% a successful LOW verification. `app/input/controller_profile.gd:553-565,568-575,588-607` applies these endpoints directly to radio input (no radio deadzone), and `app/input/rc_input.gd:240-249,357-370,417-435` may arm and then returns that nonzero value.
- Reproduce: rest capture reports roll +0.05 while the next stable center is 0.0, and throttle idle is -0.8. During the throttle sweep, inject one valid sample at -0.87, then actual full +0.8 and low -0.8. Complete roll, pitch and yaw normally. Wizard accepts the profile; its throttle is [-0.87,+0.8] and roll center is +0.05. At stable actual idle and center, after a fresh low event and poll, radio arms with throttle `0.041916` and roll `-0.058824`. The Home final FULL/LOW thresholds would accept full 1.0 and low 0.041916. A warning is not generated. This does not prove that any real JETI/EdgeTX device generates this outlier or that the aircraft accelerates by a particular amount.
- Why this is a validation issue, not a claim of perfect calibration: an instantaneous reading has unavoidable measurement uncertainty, and a deadband is a product choice. Here both a short-lived sample and a later stable, materially different neutral position satisfy the explicit confirmation, while the saved profile treats the transient as authoritative and the enabled LOW check admits the resulting nonzero throttle. No test checks stability or disagreement between the captured and confirmed neutral readings. Existing `app/tests/test_rc_calibration.gd:112-130,132-146` covers reduced range, inversion and incomplete returns with stable synthetic values, not a transient plus a later stable rest.
- Evidence: `project/audit_probe.gd` and `runs/calibration-pure-audit-aux-hlu2drsp/engine.log`, result above. Remediation: collect and evaluate a bounded stable neutral/idle window, reject/warn on significant disagreement and reject isolated endpoint spikes (using a robust/dwell-based method); do not silently apply gamepad expo or a blanket deadzone to radios. Regression: both throttle orientations, stable jitter, a single in-range endpoint outlier, biased rest followed by stable return, reduced/asymmetric ranges, and final verification/armed outputs. Hardware sample distributions and an acceptable tolerance require device evidence.

## P2 — a newly assigned flight axis that was used by an action discards the completed wizard with a generic error (high confidence)

- `app/input/controller_actions.gd:270-286` rejects flight/Pause action axes that overlap primary axes. The wizard checks collisions among flight axes and AUX, not prior semantic action axes (`app/input/rc_calibration.gd:177-192,337-353`). `app/input/controller_profile.gd:193-225` preserves action bindings, validates the final merge and returns only `{}` when one conflicts. `app/ui/controller_setup.gd:518-521` reports only “Invalid calibration. The draft is unchanged”; `app/app_root.gd:2130-2134` has already popped the wizard. The managed in-flight call likewise returns “Invalid calibration” via `app/input/controller_service.gd:361-369`.
- Reproduce: a valid prior radio profile uses axis 5 for `camera_cycle`, while primary axes use 0/1/2/3. After changing the transmitter's channel mapping, complete a valid wizard with roll on axis 5 and no other primary conflict. `Calibration.valid(measured)` is true, but the merge is empty, and direct validation says `Action camera_cycle uses primary flight axis 5`. The whole measured calibration is discarded and the pilot is not told the cause; the prior valid profile remains intact. Clearing/remapping that action manually then repeating calibration is a workaround.
- Evidence: `project/audit_probe.gd`, `runs/calibration-pure-audit-aux-hlu2drsp/engine.log`. Tests `app/tests/test_controller_profile.gd:233-253` cover newly overlapping AUX and invalid primary duplication, not a pre-existing action consuming a new flight axis. `app/tests/test_controller_audit_setup.gd` tests conflict detection during direct editing, not this merge/return path.
- Remediation: retain the measured calibration as a reviewable draft and return structured conflicts naming each action and axis, with explicit reassignment/clear choices before Save. Do not silently erase user actions. Regression: axis action and Pause conflicts; retain wizard measurements and prior active profile; resolve and save/cancel transactionally.

## P2/P3 — joystick assistant offers a calibration that cannot start, then cannot finish a new joystick setup (high confidence in call-chain, UI refusal executed)

- `app/ui/controls/setup_assistant.gd:49-55,459-463,839-856` describes a joystick as calibrated like a radio and offers its Calibrate step. Yet `app/ui/controls/setup_assistant_bridge.gd:92-101` always rejects calibration for joystick profiles and instructs use of Detect in Flight axes. The guide `docs/FIRST-LAUNCH.md:107` says joysticks skip calibration. The headless UI probe observes the refusal, and existing `app/tests/test_setup_assistant_wiring.gd:197-208` explicitly expects it while `app/tests/test_setup_assistant.gd:288-290` expects the contradictory step.
- For a newly discovered joystick, `app/ui/controller_setup.gd:2340-2349` creates `configured=false`. Choosing joystick again in the assistant leaves it unchanged (`setup_assistant_bridge.gd:49-52`); skipping calibration only advances (`setup_assistant.gd:408-412`). The assistant has no Detect or “Use this custom mapping for flight” control; its Save delegates to `_save_profile` (`setup_assistant_bridge.gd:171-177`). `_profile_candidate` at `controller_setup.gd:3854-3860` uses the still-unchecked external `joystick_enabled` and `controller_profile.gd:332-335` refuses `configured=false`. Therefore the pilot must leave the assistant and configure/enable the joystick in the main controls to complete setup. The refusal itself was executed in the minimal UI probe; full native UI flow inferred from these exact source paths, not physically exercised.
- Remediation: either implement real joystick assignment/enabling within the assistant, or skip the unsupported step and clearly route to Flight axes/enable controls before claiming Save. Regression: first-time generic joystick through the actual bridge and owner, assert valid mapping is explicitly confirmed and Save can complete; never silently enable unverified defaults.

## P3 — identical recalibration retains prior radio-menu verification (high confidence in behavior; contract interpretation)

- `docs/plans/controller-support.md:67` says recalibration invalidates radio-menu proof. `app/input/controller_profile.gd:223-225` removes `ui_navigation` only when `RadioMenu.is_verified(result)` has become false. `app/input/radio_menu.gd:14-38,52-75` defines proof through equal mapping/action fingerprints; `app/ui/controller_setup.gd:3762-3765,4089-4090` follows the same equality rule.
- Reproduce: begin with a verified profile whose throttle center is its low endpoint (wizard representation); provide a valid recalibration with exactly equal axis, endpoints, center, and inversion. The result retains `ui_navigation` and `Menu.is_verified` returns true. A changed endpoint correctly removes verification. This is a narrow breach of the stated “recalibration” rule; equality-based retention may be an intentional policy, and the synthetic result does not establish a stale physical menu mapping or unsafe navigation. Decide whether the contract should require a new physical challenge on *every* recalibration, or document/test retention for equal fingerprints.
- Evidence: `project/audit_probe.gd`, `runs/calibration-pure-audit-aux-hlu2drsp/engine.log`. Existing `app/tests/test_radio_menu_service.gd:398-423` changes the roll axis and therefore covers only the invalidating branch. Regression should test exactly unchanged measured values on both Home draft and in-flight persistence, then assert the chosen product policy and ownership/persisted preference.

## P2 — accepted V2 absolute flight channels remain absolute after radio calibration (high confidence; unusual profile prerequisite)

- `app/input/controller_profile.gd:427-473` validates either `centered` or `absolute` for every channel and only constrains range-kind to the throttle mode at lines 481-491. An existing V2 radio profile with `roll.range_kind="absolute"` thus passes validation even though roll is a signed flight command. `merge_radio_calibration` at 193-206 preserves the old `range_kind`, copying only axis, inversion, and three endpoints from the wizard's explicitly centered result. It accepts the resulting profile at line 225. `normalize_axis` at 553-565 maps an absolute channel to 0…1 regardless of its measured center. The same holds for pitch and yaw.
- Reproduce: start with a V2 `Profile.defaults("radio", "radio_custom")`, set only `roll.range_kind="absolute"`, and confirm `Profile.validate` succeeds. Feed a valid normal wizard mapping for roll min -1, center 0, max +1 to `merge_radio_calibration`. It returns a valid radio profile with the absolute range intact. Live `Profile.channel_value(0,"roll",profile)` outputs +0.5 at the centered stick, and `channel_value(-1,...)` outputs 0 at full left, instead of 0 and -1. The wizard itself previews the just-measured legacy channel through the default centered path (`calibration_view.gd:394-398`), so this discrepancy can emerge on application of the result. This case requires a previously stored, imported, or externally modified V2 profile: no normal UI control for changing `range_kind` was found. No physical-device claim is made.
- Evidence: `project/audit_probe.gd`, `runs/calibration-range-kind-oc1xiz39/{report.json,engine.log}`: `Calibration audit: 16 checks, 0 failed`; the two AUX warnings are intentional. Existing `test_controller_profile.gd:132,233-253` checks throttle range kind and metadata retention, not a persisted absolute primary surface through recalibration.
- Remediation: enforce centered roll/pitch/yaw in profile validation and migrate or explicitly repair affected accepted profiles; merge the wizard's measured range kind deliberately and validate before activation. Do not indiscriminately destroy other metadata. Regression: each signed primary axis, both kinds of inversion, V2 round trip and recalibration of previously accepted absolute channel, plus expected throttle mode.

## Dismissed or bounded candidates

- A throttle whose raw direction is reversed is not inherently mishandled: `rc_calibration.gd:195-202` chooses inversion from the low rest endpoint and `controller_profile.gd:589-595` reverses the normalized absolute output. `app/tests/test_controller_preflight_calibration.gd:209-225,240-259` covers both orientations. No device qualification is inferred.
- The in-flight wizard does not have the Home draft's separate full-to-low verification, but `docs/FIRST-LAUNCH.md:171` explicitly describes this difference. Do not report its absence as a contradiction without a stronger safety case.
- Cancel/focus/disconnect in the Home wizard are guarded by connection generation, invalid sample cancellation and unchanged draft (`radio_calibration_setup.gd:73-109,123-131`; `test_controller_preflight_calibration.gd:57-111`). In-flight focus loss defers to `app_root.gd:2903-2920,2930-2938` and cancels through `open_pause`. No general cancellation defect demonstrated.
- Radio deadzone/expo are intentionally not applied: `controller_profile.gd:568-575`; preserve this policy when fixing the neutral-estimation issue. A desirable configurable deadzone alone is not a defect.
- The Setup assistant calls the host before setting its busy flag (`setup_assistant.gd:874-889`), so a synchronously delivered completion could be missed (`:422-424`). The documented host says completion follows a started request, and no normal reachable synchronous failure was established; retain as a dismissed/unproven race, not a finding.

## Run identity and limits

Engine `/workspace/scratch/912e78c5574d/repo_current/.tools/Godot_v4.8-beta2_linux.x86_64`, version `4.8.beta2.official.cafc17a19`; pinned executable hash was verified by the lead. Both probes ran through repository `tools/godot/run_checked.py`, isolated `XDG_DATA_HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, in a disposable minimal imported project. Twenty-five copied production script/shader dependencies compared byte-for-byte equal to this baseline. The pure/aux probe intentionally logs two overlap warnings; the checked runner records them, does not promote warnings to failures, and reports success. One initial pure-probe wrapper run (`calibration-pure-audit-2_asjmeo`) had 10/10 successful checks but failed because the requested completion marker mistakenly expected 8; the corrected marker run `calibration-pure-audit-rg921z1o` passed, then the expanded 13-check run passed. A further expanded run `calibration-range-kind-oc1xiz39` passed 16 checks with the two expected AUX warnings. UI tests are headless synthetic and do not assess rendering on real displays. No full suite, physical device, native package, input latency, RF-loss behavior, or measured hardware drift was tested.
